
{"id":"0707b95a-5b75-4eef-9b2c-4c8b07d52627","object":"chat.completion","created":1785032727,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. The problem is \"DATA-HDB-02\". From the fixture payload, we see that the case contains: \"min_cluster_size\", \"clusters\" (each with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), \"parent\" mapping, and \"_case_nonce\". The task likely is to perform some hierarchical density-based clustering stability selection? The output schema has \"clusters\" array (with cluster_id, size, stability) and \"selected_cluster_ids\" array of strings.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that processes the input clusters and returns clusters with stability computed and selected_cluster_ids based on some algorithm. Looking at examples: three clusters c0 (size 7, birth 0.2, exit lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9]), c1 (size 4, birth 0.5, exits [0.7,1.0,1.2]), c2 (size 2, birth 0.8, exits [1.1]). Parent mapping: c0 root, c1 child of c0, c2 child of c0. Min_cluster_size=3.\n\nThis looks like the HDBSCAN* algorithm for hierarchical clustering stability selection. In HDBSCAN*, clusters are selected based on stability (the persistence of clusters over lambda). The stability of a cluster is defined as sum over points in cluster of (lambda_max - lambda_birth) where lambda_max is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (the exit lambda). Actually in HDBSCAN*, the stability of a cluster is sum over points in cluster of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth). The selection process: start from leaves, if sum of stabilities of children > stability of parent, then select children, else select parent.\n\nBut here we have a flat list of clusters? The \"clusters\" array includes all clusters. And \"parent\" mapping gives the hierarchy. So we need to compute stability for each cluster, then traverse the hierarchy bottom-up to select clusters. The selected_cluster_ids should be the ids of clusters that are selected by the algorithm. The output clusters should include all input clusters? Or only the ones that are selected? The output schema says \"clusters\" array with items having cluster_id, size, stability. It might be all clusters, or only the selected ones? The schema requires both \"clusters\" and \"selected_cluster_ids\". The selected_cluster_ids is a subset of cluster ids. So probably we output all input clusters with computed stability, and then the list of selected ids. However, note that the input clusters have size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas. But the output cluster object only requires cluster_id, size, stability. So we need to compute stability from those.\n\nWhat is stability? In HDBSCAN*, the stability of a cluster is: S = sum_{p in cluster} (lambda_{p,exit} - lambda_{birth}). The lambda_exit for a point is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (i.e., the next lambda after the last lambda in point_exit_lambdas? Actually point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which the point exits? In the fixture, each cluster has a list of point_exit_lambdas (presumably one per point? But size 7 has 3 exit lambdas, size 4 has 3, size 2 has 1. That doesn't match size. Hmm. Maybe point_exit_lambdas are not per point but per event? Possibly the point_exit_lambdas represent the lambda values at which points leave the cluster (sorted). The number of exit events might be less than size if some points never leave? Or maybe it's the lambda at which the cluster splits? Actually in HDBSCAN*, each point has a lambda_exit when it leaves the cluster. The birth_lambda is the lambda at which the cluster appears. The stability of a cluster is sum over points in cluster of (lambda_exit(p) - lambda_birth). For points that never leave (infinite lambda), we might set lambda_exit = infinity? But here we have finite lambdas. For a cluster of size 7, only 3 exit lambdas given. That suggests that not all points have recorded exit? Or these are the lambdas at which the cluster loses points? Possibly the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which individual points leave the cluster, but only for points that eventually leave? For points that remain until the end, lambda_exit is infinity? But in output we need to compute stability.\n\nAnother interpretation: The cluster has size, birth_lambda, and a list of point_exit_lambdas (the lambdas at which points in the cluster exit). The number of exit lambdas should equal size? Not here. Maybe the exit lambdas include only those points that exit before the cluster dies? In HDBSCAN*, a cluster dies when it splits into two or more children. The points that exit after the split are assigned to children. The point_exit_lambdas for a cluster are the lambdas at which points leave that specific cluster (not due to split but due to being \"outliers\"? Actually in HDBSCAN*, points can leave a cluster when they become noise (i.e., the cluster shrinks). The cluster's stability is defined for each cluster as the sum over points in the cluster of (lambda_max(p) - lambda_birth), where lambda_max(p) is the lambda at which point p stops being part of the cluster (either because it becomes noise or because it moves to a child cluster). The lambda_max(p) for points that are eventually in a child cluster is the lambda at which the cluster splits? Wait, the concept: In the HDBSCAN* hierarchy, each point has a lambda_max (the lambda at which it \"falls out\" of the cluster). For points that are in a cluster and eventually go into a child, their lambda_max is the lambda at which the child is formed? Actually the stability of a cluster is based on the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster. For a cluster that splits, the parent cluster's stability includes only the points that remain in the parent until the split? Not sure.\n\nGiven the limited input, we need to deduce the algorithm from the fixture. The fixture has three cases with identical payloads. The output structure expects clusters with computed stability and selected_cluster_ids. The test likely expects consistent output across the three cases (maybe due to nonce being different but payload same, output should be same). So we need to compute a deterministic answer.\n\nLet's examine the data:\nc0: size=7, birth=0.2, exit lambdas=[0.4,0.6,0.9] (3 values). Children: c1 (size4, birth0.5), c2 (size2, birth0.8).\nc1: size=4, exit lambdas=[0.7,1.0,1.2] (3 values). No children? Parent c0.\nc2: size=2, exit lambdas=[1.1] (1 value). No children. Parent c0.\n\nMin_cluster_size=3. So clusters smaller than 3 are not considered? In HDBSCAN*, clusters with size below min_cluster_size are not considered as clusters; they are considered noise? But here we have c2 with size2 (<3). So possibly c2 is not a valid cluster? The algorithm might exclude it from selection.\n\nThe parent mapping shows c2 parent is c0, so c2 is a child cluster that splits from c0? But its size is 2, which is less than min_cluster_size. In HDBSCAN*, clusters with size below min_cluster_size are not valid clusters and are considered as \"let the points be assigned to parent\"? Actually the algorithm: cluster selection is done only on clusters with size >= min_cluster_size. Children that are too small are not considered as separate clusters; their points are considered part of the parent cluster. So the selection process may ignore c2.\n\nNow compute stability for each cluster. The stability sum_{p in cluster} (lambda_exit(p) - lambda_birth). The lambda_exit for each point is given by point_exit_lambdas? But the number of lambdas is less than size, so we need to assume that for points not listed, they have lambda_exit = infinity? Or maybe the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which the cluster loses points (e.g., due to points leaving to noise or child). For a cluster, the lambda_exit for points that stay until the end of the cluster (i.e., points that are in the cluster at lambda = infinity) would be infinity. But stability calculation uses lambda_exit for each point; if lambda_exit is infinite, then (inf - birth) is infinite, which would give infinite stability. That doesn't make sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points leave the cluster to form child clusters? For c0, the birth is 0.2, and it has children c1 (birth 0.5) and c2 (birth 0.8). The point_exit_lambdas for c0: 0.4,0.6,0.9. The children's birth lambdas are 0.5 and 0.8. So the exit lambdas possibly correspond to points leaving c0 to become noise? Or to join children? The children's birth lambdas are the lambdas at which those clusters appear, meaning that at those lambdas, points from c0 move to the child cluster. In the HDBSCAN* hierarchy, a parent cluster's points that go to a child cluster have lambda_exit from the parent equal to the child's birth lambda. So for c0, points that eventually end up in c1 would have lambda_exit = 0.5, and points that end up in c2 have lambda_exit = 0.8. Points that stay in c0 until the end (if any) have lambda_exit = infinity. But here c0 size=7, children total size 4+2=6, so one point remains in c0? That point would have no exit lambda. But the point_exit_lambdas list has three numbers: 0.4,0.6,0.9. None match 0.5 or 0.8. So maybe the exit lambdas are for points that leave the hierarchy entirely (become noise). In HDBSCAN*, points can also leave the cluster as noise (when the cluster shrinks). The lambdas 0.4,0.6,0.9 could be lambda values at which points become noise directly from c0. So those points are not in any child. That would account for 3 points. Then the remaining points: 7 - 3 = 4 points, but children have total 6 points, that's inconsistent. Let's recalc: c0 size=7. If 3 become noise, 4 remain. But c1 has size=4, c2 size=2, total 6 children. So 4 children would imply 4 points go to children, but that would require 2 more points? Actually parent points: size 7 total. 3 become noise -> 4 left. But children total 6, so at least 2 more points must come from somewhere? Not possible. So maybe the point_exit_lambdas are not per point but are the lambda values at which the cluster's connectivity changes? Hmm.\n\nMaybe we misinterpret: The point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points exit the *cluster* (i.e., the cluster loses a point). The cluster may have children, but the points that go to children do not exit the cluster; they are still part of the cluster? Actually in the hierarchy, when a child cluster forms, the points that form the child are still considered part of the parent cluster? In HDBSCAN*, the cluster persistence is defined differently: the stability of a cluster is sum over points in the cluster of (lambda_max - lambda_min) where lambda_min is the lambda at which the point becomes part of the cluster (when it is born) and lambda_max is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster (either by becoming noise or by going to a child cluster). For points that go to a child cluster, the lambda_max is the child's birth lambda (the split). So such points do exit the parent cluster. So for c0, points that go to c1 have lambda_exit=0.5, points to c2 have lambda_exit=0.8. That accounts for 6 points. Then the remaining point (size 7-6=1) might stay in c0 until infinite? So that point has lambda_exit=∞. But the point_exit_lambdas for c0 have three values: 0.4,0.6,0.9. These could be the lambdas at which other points become noise directly from c0 (not going to children). That would be 3 noise points. Then total points: 3 noise + 6 children = 9 > 7. So inconsistent.\n\nGiven the confusion, we need to look for a known pattern. This might be a simplified version: The stability of a cluster is computed as sum of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth) for each point in point_exit_lambdas? Or maybe it's the difference between successive lambdas? Or perhaps the \"point_exit_lambdas\" actually represent the lambdas at which the cluster splits into subclusters? The number of values might correspond to the number of children? c0 has 2 children, but 3 exit lambdas; c1 has no children, but 3 exit lambdas; c2 has no children, 1 exit lambda. So that doesn't match.\n\nMaybe the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points exit the cluster to become noise. In c1, size=4, exit lambdas=[0.7,1.0,1.2] (3 values). So 3 points become noise, 1 remains? In c2, size=2, exit lambdas=[1.1] (1 value), so 1 point becomes noise, 1 remains. In c0, size=7, exit lambdas=[0.4,0.6,0.9] (3), so 3 become noise, 4 remain. But then children have 6 points, which is more than the 4 remaining, so something's off.\n\nMaybe the point_exit_lambdas are not for noise events but for the cluster's persistence? Could be that each cluster's stability is simply the sum of the point_exit_lambdas minus size*birth? Or some formula.\n\nWait, the output schema expects \"stability\" as a number. In the fixture, there is no expected output given. So we need to infer the algorithm from task name \"DATA-HDB-02\". HDB likely stands for HDBSCAN. The \"02\" might refer to cluster selection step. The input includes min_cluster_size, clusters with birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas, and parent mapping. The typical HDBSCAN* cluster selection algorithm: For each cluster, compute stability = sum_{p in cluster} (lambda_max(p) - lambda_min(p)). Then traverse tree bottom-up: for each node, if stability_sum of children > stability of node, then select children; else select node. For clusters with size < min_cluster_size, they are considered as part of parent (not selected separately). Also, the final selected clusters are those that are selected in the traversal and have size >= min_cluster_size? Possibly.\n\nLet's outline the algorithm based on standard HDBSCAN* cluster selection from the paper (AP: \"HDBSCAN: Hierarchical density based clustering\").\n\nGiven:\n- Cluster objects: each has cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas? Actually in HDBSCAN*, each point has a lambda value at which it becomes noise (lambda_exit). The cluster's stability is sum over its points of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth). The lambda_exit for a point is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (becomes noise). If the point is in a child cluster, its lambda_exit from the parent is the child's birth_lambda? But that would be used in parent stability? Actually in the algorithm, when a cluster splits, the parent cluster's stability does not include the points that go to children; those points are considered part of the children clusters for stability. The parent's stability is only for points that remain in the parent until the end (i.e., become noise at some point). So the parent's stability is based on the points that never go to any child (i.e., noise points within the parent). That matches: the point_exit_lambdas for a cluster are the lambdas at which points in that cluster become noise (i.e., leave the cluster permanently). Points that go to children are not considered in the parent's stability; they are assigned to the child. So the size of a cluster might include points that will later go to children? Actually in the hierarchy, the parent cluster exists from its birth until its death (when it splits). At the split, the parent cluster is replaced by children. So all points in the parent eventually either become noise at some point before the split, or go to a child. The points that go to children are not considered in the parent's stability because they are part of the children. So the stability of a cluster is computed only from points that become noise while in that cluster (i.e., exit lambdas within the cluster's existence). That explains why point_exit_lambdas count may be less than size.\n\nThus:\n- For a cluster, the \"size\" is the number of points that are in the cluster at its birth? Or the total number of points that ever belong to that cluster? In our data, c0 size=7, c1 size=4, c2 size=2. c1 and c2 are children of c0, so the total points in the tree is 7 (since c1 and c2 are subsets of c0). Actually, c0 has 7 points. Of those, 3 become noise in c0 (exit lambdas 0.4,0.6,0.9). The remaining 4 points go to children? But c1 and c2 together have 6 points, which is more than 4, so that can't be. Unless the sizes are cumulative? Not.\n\nMaybe the sizes are not the number of points, but the number of points that are in that cluster at its maximum? In HDBSCAN*, the hierarchy shows clusters as connected components at different lambda levels. The size of a cluster is the number of points in it at the time of its birth. For c0, birth 0.2, size 7. For c1, birth 0.5, size 4 (points that form c1 at that lambda). For c2, birth 0.8, size 2. So total points in children = 6, but parent had 7, so one point must have been noise before 0.5. Indeed, exit lambda 0.4 is one point that becomes noise at 0.4, before c1 is born at 0.5. Then at 0.5, the remaining 6 points? But c1 size is 4, c2 size is 2, total 6, that works. But then the exit lambdas for c0 include 0.6 and 0.9 after c1 and c2 births? That would be noise points from children? Actually after c1 and c2 are formed, the points are in children, not in c0. So c0 no longer exists after its split. So exit lambdas for c0 should only be before the split. The split happens at the child's birth. At 0.5, c0 splits into c1 and maybe some points left? But c2 is born at 0.8, so c0 splits at 0.5 into two groups: one becomes c1 (size4) and the remaining? Possibly the remaining 2 points are noise? Wait, no: at 0.5, the hierarchy: c0 exists up to 0.5, then it splits into c1 and some other component that later becomes c2 at 0.8? Actually c2 is another child of c0, but its birth is 0.8, so there might be another split. In HDBSCAN*, a parent can split into multiple children at different lambdas. So c0 exists until 0.8? Or until the first child? Typically, when a cluster splits, the parent ends and multiple children start. But here c2 is born later, so maybe c0 first splits into c1 and a temporary cluster that later becomes c2. The point_exit_lambdas of c0 could represent noise events from that temporary cluster. But we don't have enough info.\n\nGiven the complexity, and the fact that this is a synthetic test, there might be a simpler, deterministic formula for stability and selection based on the given data. Possibly:\n- The stability of a cluster is the sum of (point_exit_lambdas) minus (size * birth_lambda)? That would be: for c0: sum(0.4,0.6,0.9)=1.9, size* birth=7*0.2=1.4, stability=0.5.\n- For c1: sum(0.7,1.0,1.2)=2.9, size*birth=4*0.5=2.0, stability=0.9.\n- For c2: sum(1.1)=1.1, size*birth=2*0.8=1.6, stability=-0.5 (negative).\n\nBut stability shouldn't be negative. Alternatively, sum(lambda_exit) per point, not summed? The stability is sum over points of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth). So if we had per-point exit lambdas, we would subtract birth for each. If we know number of exit events (points that became noise), then for those points, contribution = lambda_exit - birth. For points that go to children, they don't contribute to parent's stability. But we don't know which points go to children. However, we can infer: total points in cluster size. Number of exit events given. The remaining points (size - len(exit_lambdas)) are points that go to children. For parent stability, only exit events count. So parent stability = sum(exit_lambdas) - (number_of_exit_points)*birth_lambda. For c0: 3 exit points, sum=1.9, birth=0.2 => contribution = 1.9 - 3*0.2 = 1.9 - 0.6 = 1.3. For c1: 3 exit points, sum=2.9, birth=0.5 => 2.9 - 1.5 = 1.4. For c2: 1 exit point, sum=1.1, birth=0.8 => 0.3. That gives positive stabilities. But then what about points that go to children? They contribute to child stabilities, not parent. So total stability for the tree is sum of parent and children stabilities.\n\nBut the output requires stability for each cluster, so we can compute that. Then selection: we need to decide which clusters to select. According to HDBSCAN*, we do a bottom-up traversal, comparing sum of child stabilities to parent stability. If children have higher total stability, select children; else select parent. But we must also consider that clusters with size < min_cluster_size are not selected (they are considered as part of parent). So c2 size 2 < 3, so it should not be selected, and its points are part of parent. That means when comparing c0 and its children, we only consider valid children (size >= min_cluster_size). c1 is valid (size4), c2 is not. So the comparison is between c0 stability and c1's stability (since c2 is ignored). Also, the stability of c2 would be disregarded? But for c2, its points are considered as part of c0? Actually, if c2 is too small, it's not a separate cluster, its points are considered as noise in the parent? Or they contribute to parent's stability? The parent c0 already has its own noise points (exit lambdas). But points that go to c2 are not in c0's exit lambdas; they are in c2. However, since c2 is not selected, those points should be considered as part of c0's noise? Hmm.\n\nIn standard HDBSCAN* implementation (e.g., scikit-learn-contrib/hdbscan), the cluster selection algorithm works on the condensed tree. Clusters with fewer than min_cluster_size points are considered as noise and are not represented; their points are absorbed into the parent. So in the condensed tree, clusters with size < min_cluster_size are not nodes. So for our data, c2 would be removed from the tree, and its points would be considered as exiting from c0 (with lambda_exit equal to c2's birth? Or maybe they become noise at c2's birth?). The condensed tree construction merges small clusters back into their parent. So we need to simulate that.\n\nGiven the minimal information, perhaps the intended solution is simply:\n- For each cluster, compute stability as: sum over its point_exit_lambdas (or using the given list) minus (birth_lambda * len(point_exit_lambdas))? Actually the number of exit points is len(point_exit_lambdas). But the size of cluster is given; we need to account for points that do not exit (i.e., stay indefinitely). In the stability definition, if a point never exits, its lambda_exit is infinite, so stability contribution is infinite. That would make infinite stability, which is not useful. So likely, the point_exit_lambdas list includes all points that exit the cluster (become noise). Points that go to children are not in this list. So for stability, we only consider the points that exit. The size includes all points originally, but we ignore those that go to children for parent stability. So stability = sum(point_exit_lambdas) - birth_lambda * len(point_exit_lambdas). That seems plausible.\n\nNow for selection: Traverse the tree bottom-up. For each node, create a list of children that are valid (size >= min_cluster_size). For each valid child, compute its stability (as above). Then, if the sum of stabilities of valid children > node's stability, then select the children (and recursively select from children); else select the node. For nodes that are not valid (size < min_cluster_size), they are ignored and their stability is not used; their points are folded into parent.\n\nIn our tree: c0 (size7, valid), c1 (size4, valid), c2 (size2, invalid). So only c1 is a valid child. Compute stabilities: c0: stability = 1.9 - 3*0.2 = 1.3; c1: 2.9 - 3*0.5 = 1.4. Since c1's stability (1.4) > c0's (1.3), we select c1. c0 is not selected. c2 is invalid, so not selected. So selected_cluster_ids = [\"c1\"].\n\nBut wait, what about c2's points? They are not selected. The output clusters array should include all clusters? Or only selected? The schema says \"clusters\" array with cluster_id, size, stability. It likely includes all clusters (input clusters) with computed stability, regardless of selection. Because selected_cluster_ids is a separate array. So we output all clusters: c0, c1, c2 with their computed stabilities. For c2, stability computed as above: sum(1.1) - 1*0.8 = 0.3. But c2 size is less than min_cluster_size, but its stability is still computed. That is fine.\n\nSo for the given fixture, the output for each case (nonce differs but payload same) would be same: clusters: [ {cluster_id:\"c0\", size:7, stability:1.3}, {cluster_id:\"c1\", size:4, stability:1.4}, {cluster_id:\"c2\", size:2, stability:0.3} ], selected_cluster_ids: [\"c1\"].\n\nBut we need to verify if this matches typical HDBSCAN* behavior. In standard HDBSCAN*, the stability for a cluster is computed as sum of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth) over all points that belong to that cluster at its maximum extent. That includes points that later go to children? I think not. The stability of a cluster is based on the points that are \"born\" in that cluster and eventually \"die\" (become noise) without going to a child. For points that go to children, they are not part of the parent's stability; they are part of child's stability. So our computation is consistent with that.\n\nWe must also consider that for the root cluster (c0), the birth_lambda is 0.2, and its points that become noise (3 points) have exit lambdas. The points that go to children (4 points) are accounted in children's stabilities. But children may also have noise points. So total stabilities sum should be sum over all points of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth) where lambda_exit is the final noise lambda for each point (ultimate death). That would be: c0 noise points: (0.4-0.2)+(0.6-0.2)+(0.9-0.2)=0.2+0.4+0.7=1.3. c1 noise points: (0.7-0.5)+(1.0-0.5)+(1.2-0.5)=0.2+0.5+0.7=1.4. c2 noise point: (1.1-0.8)=0.3. Total=3.0. That works.\n\nNow, c2 is a child of c0, but its size is 2 (<3). In the condensed tree, it is considered as noise rather than a separate cluster. The algorithm should treat it as part of its parent. That means the points of c2 (which have exit lambda 1.1) should be considered as noise from the parent c0. But in our computation, we gave c2 its own stability. However, if c2 is not a valid cluster, its points are absorbed into c0. That would ch